A||||14||Hawkman||Radio wiring question||||5||
Z||000000||Hawkman||06-14-2000||12:31 PM||||I'm replacing my stock GM AM/FM/Cassette player with an after-market CD player.  No problem getting an adapter kit for the dash.<P>There is an after-market wiring harness available for GM vehicles (can't remember which years are covered) but the Fiero is not listed.  Is it because of the left & right speakers mounted in each seat that makes the otherwise GM "stock" adapter not work?<P>Can I modify a standard GM wiring harness to work in my 85 coupe?<P>Any suggestions?<P>Thanks!||||reg||5
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||06-14-2000||12:40 PM||||<BR>Hey, Hawk I have in the mall a WANTED post regarding OE stereo, are you wanting to sell yours, I may be a willing buyer.<P>Here is the link: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum4/HTML/000887.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum4/HTML/000887.html</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Hawkman||06-14-2000||12:50 PM||||Hey Gold-86SE,<P>I am willing to sell my stock radio but it's just the "plain-jane" stock AM/FM/Cassette.  Unfortunately it does not have the extra's you listed in your want ad.<P>Sorry...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||06-14-2000||12:53 PM||||I installed a Jensen AM/FM/CD/Cassette unit in my 85SE.  I got the standard GM wiring adapter for that year for about $8.00 from Walmart and everything works fine.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Gold-86SE||06-14-2000||02:16 PM||||<BR>Hawk, thanks for letting me know. You never know until you ask. Thanks again.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phaeton||06-14-2000||11:04 PM||||Fiero's have the GM 12 pin plug.  When most of the GM line changed to 21 pin, The Fiero did not change.<P>Did they know ahead of time the Fiero was cancelled?  Hmmmmm||||reg||12
Z||000006||Hawkman||06-15-2000||08:38 AM||||As it turns out I bought a "rampage" wiring harness from Kmart for $6.99.  I realized after I took the stock stereo out that the "standard" GM wiring harness that works for most GM vehicle didn't even come close to matching what came out.  There are 4 separate connectors (3 of them together)and are spliced onto the stock wiring. Maybe the stereo was replaced with an other-than-stock Fiero stereo.  <P>The bottom line is that I'll need to determine which wires do what that come from behind the dashboard. <P>Where can I find a stereo wiring diagram for an 85 coupe?<P>Thanks to all that have responded!||||reg||1
Z||000007||tgowens||06-15-2000||05:17 PM||||Hawkman, the Haynes manual has a wiring diagram for the radio showing color codes and component wiring.......||||reg||1
Z||000008||GT-maniac||06-16-2000||09:13 PM||||Unfortunately that diagram isn't terribly usefull for those of us with speakers in the seats as it only shows two rear speakers and we have four in the rear. Two per headrest.<BR>I finally figured out which wires did what by trial and error on mine so if you decide not to go with the wiring harness and just splice them in let me know and I can tell you which wires do what. BTW I love the speakers in the headrest idea it's too bad Pontiac did away with them in the later fiero's. Though I can see that they would be a pain to replace or upgrade as you'd have to cut open the seat to get them out. Fortunately mine are in great shape.   <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||06-17-2000||09:23 AM||||The speakers in the seats are only good if you're 4 feet tall.  Otherwise they drive into your shoulders instead of your ears.  It's too bad because there are some 3 1/2" speakers now from some pretty reputable manufacturers.||||reg||1
Z||000010||ounmongk||06-17-2000||07:40 PM||||Hawk, If there is a Best Buy store around where you are; go into the installation bay and ask to purchase a wiring harness. Makes the job A LOT easier  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> BTW, what brand mounting bracket did you use? I went with a Radio Shack "quality" mounting bracket, and it turned an hour-and-a-half installation into a three-hour long project. Good luck. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fiasco||06-18-2000||11:24 PM||||I have the headrest spkrs, too, and I used the GM harness adapter. Worked fine.||||reg||1
Z||000012||286SE'S||06-19-2000||06:25 PM||||If you replace the seat speakers you should be aware that the original speakers are 10 ohms and are run in parallel. While recovering my seats with Mr Mikes leather, I replaced mine  with 4 ohm Pyle speakers and within a week fried the amplifier chip for the right side in the stock radio. It was red hot! The 4 ohm loads in parallel was a 2 ohm load to the  amp, which it was not designed to drive. I changed the harness under the glovebox console to have them run in parallel. They are a little lower in volume but work great.<BR>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000013||286SE'S||06-19-2000||06:27 PM||||OOps! I meant rewired them in series!<BR>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000014||Hawkman||06-19-2000||06:34 PM||||Thanks for all the great info folks!<P>I bought the Rampage brand installation kit for the radio too.  It is a fairly lightweight plastic job but looks good installed.  It's one of the generic models for all GM cars and is snapped together using a variety of small plastic mounting parts.  Hey, what can I expect for ten bucks?<P>I didn't know about the 10 ohm speakers in the seats.  That may pose a challenge but should work.  I like the speakers in the seat too but they seem a touch frail.<P>I'll let you know how things turn out ...||||reg||7
